ad8ed", "message": "programming2.cpp", "created_at": "2024-10-19T17:09:10Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "1ad952f092debb6f03dc876ee9525ffbcfa36a17", "message": "Update programming1.cpp", "created_at": "2024-10-19T17:06:53Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "27638343463ee079c3583a9a84dc6aa60e4276bd", "message": "Update functions.cpp\n\nFunctions in C++ are blocks of code that perform a specific task, allowing for modular, reusable, and organized programming. They enable code to be broken down into smaller, manageable pieces, making it easier to read, maintain, and debug. Each function has a name, return type, and parameters (if any).\r\n\r\nKey Points:\r\n\r\nFunction Definition: Specifies the code to be executed. Consists of a return type, function name, parameter list, and body.\r\n\r\nFunction Declaration (Prototype): Declares a function without providing its implementation, informing the compiler about its signature.\r\n\r\nFunction Call: Executes a defined function by passing arguments that match the parameter types.\r\n\r\nReturn Types: Functions can return a value (e.g., int, double) or void if no value is returned.\r\n\r\nParameter Passing: Supports passing by value (copy), reference (original object), and pointer.\r\n\r\nFunction Overloading: Allows multiple functions with the same name but different parameter lists.\r\n\r\nDefault Parameters: Provides default values for function parameters.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe repository file covers function creation, calling conventions, best practices, and advanced topics like recursion and lambda functions. This guide aims to enhance understanding of function utilization in C++ programs.", "created_at": "2024-10-19T17:05:48Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "9bf9acd8ba7e4ad2acd3e30d36a258f1b6621740", "message": "Merge pull request #2 from KUMARANKIT3012/KUMARANKIT3012-DSA\n\nAdd files via upload", "created_at": "2024-10-18T18:03:09Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "aa6bb60231ce6b8c1641982fae497b2eb3ece172", "message": "Add files via upload\n\nFunctions file:\r\nFunction Files are essential components of C++ programming that encapsulate reusable code blocks, promoting modularity and organization. This summary highlights key aspects of function files:\r\n\r\n1. Introduction to Functions:\r\n\r\nFunctions are named blocks of code that perform specific tasks.\r\nThey improve code readability, reusability, and maintainability.\r\nFunctions can take input parameters and return values.\r\n2. Return Type:\r\n\r\nFunctions can return values of various data types (e.g., int, float, char, bool, custom data structures).\r\nIf a function doesn't return a value, its return type is void.\r\n3. Forward Declaration:\r\n\r\nA function prototype is declared before its definition, informing the compiler about the function's signature.\r\nThis allows functions to call each other without knowing the implementation details.\r\n4. Sample Problems:\r\n\r\nPrime Number: Implement a function to check if a given number is prime.\r\nnCr Problem: Create a function to calculate the combination (nCr) of two numbers.\r\nFibonacci Sequence: Write a function to generate the Fibonacci sequence up to a given term.\r\n5. Call by Value and Call by Reference:\r\n\r\nCall by Value: Copies the value of arguments into the function's parameters. Changes made inside the function do not affect the original values.\r\nCall by Reference: Passes the address of arguments to the function's parameters. Changes made inside the function affect the original values.\r\n6. Call Stack:\r\n\r\nFunctions are stored on a stack during execution.\r\nWhen a function is called, its activation record (containing parameters, return address, and local variables) is pushed onto the stack.\r\nWhen a function returns, its activation record is popped off the stack.\r\n7. Trailing Zeros:\r\n\r\nA function can be implemented to count the number of trailing zeros in the factorial of a given number.\r\nAdditional Notes:\r\n\r\nFunctions can be overloaded, allowing multiple functions with the same name but different parameters.\r\nFunction templates provide a way to create generic functions that can work with different data types.\r\nBy understanding these concepts, you can effectively use function files to write well-structured, efficient, and maintainable C++ programs.", "created_at": "2024-10-18T17:55:21Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "e6475793570fc8fef7104348315914575642aedf", "message": "Add files via upload", "created_at": "2024-10-17T13:11:27Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "f3cb64b6204670d4041e0f1740da5813c16d1b73", "message": "Add files via upload", "created_at": "2024-10-16T03:05:19Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "2311378e88c041964a09ae5b42c1fa754f81aeaa", "message": "Add files via upload", "created_at": "2024-10-11T14:33:28Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "23558b022245aa599ebd66c4872960e979306552", "message": "Add files via upload\n\npro level pattern printing questions \ud83d\udd25\ud83d\udd25", "created_at": "2024-09-19T14:00:53Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "da9e7e350c5a990718e3cb7bf727da4a7299ff8f", "message": "Add files via upload\n\nAdvanced level pattern printing problems \r\nIncluding concepts of space.......", "created_at": "2024-09-17T07:29:53Z", "repo": "CODINGBOT", "file_sampled": false, "has_test_file": null, "has_impl_file": null}, {"sha": "0f226c28665c29cd62d4f1ea9a4cd067931802ed"